<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollonia (Cyrenaica)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sozousa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apollonia was the ancient port of Cyrene in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, at modern Marsa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. It grew from Cyrene's harbour into one of the five cities of the Pentapolis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, and in late Roman and Byzantine times, renamed Sozousa, it became the leading city of the province. Much of its harbour now lies under the sea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apollonia-cyrenaica.jpg" alt="Apollonia (Cyrenaica)" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ursus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apollonia_portoriental.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Apollonia was founded by Greek colonists and served as the harbour of Cyrene, 20 kilometres to the south-west. It became a significant commercial centre of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and was independent of Cyrene at the latest by the time the region came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, when it counted among the five cities of the Libyan Pentapolis. Its power kept growing until in the 6th century CE it became the capital of the Roman province of Libya Superior, or Libya Pentapolitana.</p><p>The earthquake and tsunami centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> on 21 July 365 apparently did great damage to the city and its harbour. Known by then as Sozusa, it was the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and it remains a titular see of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. Urban life came to an end after the Arab occupation of 643, and the modern settlement of Marsa Susa, which keeps the late name, grew up long afterwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Earthquakes have sunk the city's early foundations below sea level, while the Byzantine layers stand several metres above it on the deposits of earlier ages. Beechey recorded buildings in the sea, with rough drawings, in 1827, and in 1958 and 1959 Nicholas Flemming, then a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> undergraduate, led teams of divers who mapped the large sector of the city under water. The ancient port, from the 6th or 7th century BCE and now about 3.7 metres down, is exceptionally well preserved; no other complete port is as old.</p><p>On land stands the Palace of the Dux, excavated by Goodchild between 1959 and 1962, with more than 100 rooms, last used as the residence of the Byzantine duke. The Greek theatre outside the old walls, facing the sea, dates from the 3rd century BCE, was altered under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a> for gladiatorial fights and has 28 rows of seats. The Apollonia (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>) Museum keeps many of the finds.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apollonia (Cyrenaica)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The city remained the leading power of Cyrenaica, a group of Greek cities later called the Pentapolis, together with Barca, Euesperides, Taucheira and its own port, <b>Apollonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-cyrene">Republic of Cyrene</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek cities of the Pentapolis, Cyrene, <b>Apollonia</b>, Ptolemais, Taucheira and Berenice, kept Greek language and institutions.</blockquote><a href="/state/roman-cyrenaica">Roman Cyrenaica</a></li></ul></section>
